and we're joined tonight by kate andrews  economics correspondent and spectator  and sebastian payne whitehall editor of  the financial times welcome to you both  good evening let's start  with the the guardian  and their headline russia escalates war  in ukraine with attack close to nato  border  and this is uh the attack just some uh  15 miles we believe from the border with  poland an escalation of the uh the  attack on on ukraine and the  analysis has been that actually it's  it's close to an attack on nato  sebastian  yes it's clearly a warning shot russia  have done um to obviously try and  threaten and intimidate the west  particularly um the nato countries who  have been putting many weapons and goods  to help the ukrainian war effort and  this has been a very deadly attack  according to the guardian tomorrow um 20  at least 35 people have been killed in  134 injured this really was pretty  significant and as i said it was very  much designed to intimidate and suggest  the fact that this is not going to plan  and they want to try and stop the supply  of weapons but i think in fact it will  have the opposite effect because i think  many of the western countries have seen  that this has not gone to plan for  president vladimir putin and if you look  at the statement from boris johnson who  spoke to president zielinski of ukraine  this afternoon and the west is still  looking at trying to get more equipment  out to ukraine be it things like  generators be weapons whatever it is to  try and help that particular war effort  so it sort of smacks a bit of  desperation but of course as has been  written about extensively over the past  couple of weeks having a leader like  putin who um it does not necessarily  operate the normal bounds and is happy  to try and push things and push that  madman theory forward is not a  comfortable place to be but i think for  the west following this incident it's  times to calm hold their nerves and keep  folks on the strategy which is to supply  the ukrainian forces not get directly  involved and crucially not get involved  in a tit-for-tat with russia because  that's something that could escalate  very quickly and president joe biden of  the u.s said this weekend that's the one  thing he doesn't want to happen so if  putin was trying to trigger some  response i don't think you're  necessarily going to see it from this  awful attack although we have heard from  the the u.s national security adviser  jake sullivan uh quoted in the guardian  warning that any fire even accidental on  a neighboring nato country would trigger  a full force nato response kate what  whatever that means  well this is why putin's attack on the  military base just 15 miles from the  polish border is so worrying because  as we have discovered over the past few  weeks we just don't know how far he  would go and attacking one nato country  is seen under the treaty as attacking  all nato countries it's very difficult  to define what full force would mean  there it still seems that most western  countries are not interested on in a  boots on the ground approach as  sebastian they want to keep supplying  ukrainian  forces with weapons but they do not want  to actually intervene themselves the  question now is is how far putin will  push that theory and does he want  that theory does he want to test the  west when he has tested the west over  the past few weeks it has only united  these european countries that were seen  to be on very taking very different  approaches uh even just a month ago you  now have countries like germany and even  sweden pledging to up their defense  spending something that  you would not have expected to see mere  weeks ago so so far it has created unity  but you know these are these are  obviously very troubling signs from  putin that he's getting so close to the  polish border and of course it's it's  also very close to the city of lev in  the western ukraine which up until very  very recently was seen as a place where  you weren't getting those full-scale  attacks that we've seen in other parts  of ukraine the fact that putin is  pushing his troops even further into  ukraine is very worrying for the people  who of course are still there still  fighting and taking cover  and sebastian we have heard from russia  that this is a a deliberate uh target  that they are targeting those that are a  corridor to uh in terms of military um  assistance to ukraine so it's not it's  not by chance and this is perhaps  something that that will be pursued  further in future  well indeed and just to come back  briefly to your earlier point about the  potential attack on a nato country the  key thing that we've seen from putin's  strategy so far is to push the line but  not to go over it so he knew by  attacking your base 15 miles from the  border exactly what message would send  but it was not an attack on poland which  as jake sullivan from the binding  administration said that would list a  full article five response for nato  which means attack on one is an attack  on all the members so i think putin will  keep doing that but don't forget he went  into this war he invaded ukraine with a  specific purpose of trying to  demilitarize the country that's what  they've said in this so-called special  operation is he describes it in an  invasion of wars the rest of the world  has described it so it's no surprise  they're going after these military bases  but we shouldn't forget that that is  obviously a part of the era to try and  win this war which they are struggling  to do but the attacks on civilians are  absolutely appalling and again we've  seen more images over this weekend out  of kiev out of other cities and it's  clear that first of all that there are  war crimes going on here and the  civilian population is being  specifically talented by russian troops  but also that you know this is a  devastating effect this is having on  ukraine the fact is that kiev was a city  of four million people it's not up to  two million people its population and  some of the images and reporting  suggests it's become a total ghost town  and it's only going to get worse  uh but but also clear um kate that uh  this attack isn't going to deter the  west offering help to ukraine that  that's absolutely clear isn't it  so far it only seems that the the  further putin goes the more the west is  willing to respond by supplying the  ukrainian forces with more weapons with  more cash with more support and  humanitarian aid  of course but also you you have seen  those economic sanctions which are  really starting to crush the russian  economy those reports out of moscow  about you know the economy really  tutoring on collapse people's bank cards  no longer work they can't scan into the  two because apple pay has stopped  working um you know the daily lives of  russians are are being um severely  impacted now which is obviously uh going  to have an impact  we've had thousands of people arrested  in russia for actively protesting so  that there you know part of that will be  a moral perspective on the part of many  russians who are just not happy about  what putin is doing in ukraine uh part  of that will be frustration that their  normal lives are collapsing and it has  taken the uk some time to get its head  around sanctions against individual  oligarchs but you can see almost day by  day uh western countries including the  uk are making advances there they are  willing to go further there are more  steps that the west could take if it  wanted to [ __ ] russia's economy and  it very likely will continue to do so so  so far putin's actions have only  continued to unite the west as sebastian  notes he may be trying to test that  going so close to the polish border with  this latest attack on the military base  he may be trying to see what breaks the  west in their unity so far he hasn't  been able to find that crack all of his  actions have have brought europe and  the uk and america together in a way  that really was never expected to be so  united just weeks ago  and all the while worth noting that uh  peace talks are continuing between  ukraine and uh russia and uh positive  soundings are coming out of those talks  at the moment let's have a look at the  guardian uh sebastian and take us into  this story the former u.n secretary  general ban ki-moon has an environmental  warning for the uk  yes there's been a huge amount of  chatter in westminster over the past  couple of weeks about fracking now the  um sort of digging up of shale gas was  something the uk has explored but it's  all come to quite a bitter end and  there's no all the [ __ ] sites have  been concreted up and among  environmental concerns practical  concerns economic concerns about whether  this was going to be a viable form of  energy production here now it's come  back on the agenda because following the  ukraine crisis and obviously the fact  that uk and the us have um are stopping  buying oil from russia there's been a  lot of pressure on boris johnson to  reinvigorate fracking and some people in  government like the business secretary  quasi quattang have said this is not  something you're looking to it's going  to take 10 years it still might not be  viable viable and it's very difficult to  do in the uk which is a very densely  populated country but then other people  in the tory party say well it works very  well in america we need to get on this  and we need to focus on energy security  not net zero and in this common funky  moon says you know but a very dangerous  point that we should not resile from the  net zero ambition but we can't look at  it doing through fracking which as i  said some people allege is dangerous and  not sustainable but i think it is a very  interesting debate because the uk did  try this there was a moment where  fracking seemed to be a sort of hail  mary pass for the uk's energy production  that now seems to pull back so i imagine  there'll be a lot of noise about this  from mps and number 10 but i'd be very  surprised if it actually goes anywhere  not least due to the objections of local  residents as we saw when fracking was  tried in lancashire last time and came  to a very abrupt end  kate let's uh turn to um the telegraph  we've just got time for this story um  dominic cumberbatch at the uh baftas has  said that he's going to be willing or  he's actually going to go ahead and  house a ukrainian refugee given the  opportunity  yeah so the baptist tonight benedict  cumberbatch has announced that he will  look into hosting a ukrainian refugee  which is something that theoretically  many of us will be able to do here in  the uk when the homes for ukraine scheme  comes into action which has been um now  announced by michael gove the leveling  up secretary there's still a lot of  questions around the scheme the  telegraph reports that they're going to  be there's going to be a lot of  bureaucracy around a lot of hurdles to  jump over people who are hosting  ukrainians are going to go uh through  background checks um there's going to be  uh you have to commit to doing it for at  least six months there's still a lot  more red tape in the uk even with this  new and improved scheme from where we  were a few days ago than there is in the  rest of europe so one does have to  wonder how many extra ukrainian refugees  this will actually allow to be host in  the uk um but this isn't we're in a much  better situation at least now with the  government having innovative uh policy  solutions as to how we can help more  refugees than we were just a few days  ago indeed but as you say uh many  questions still to be answered and  policy to be ironed out i.e how people  will be supported if they do take in  refugees

